1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,211
This rubric isn't a new standard.

2
00:00:02,211 --> 00:00:06,496
It's the same five checkpoints from Section 11.1, read against what actually

3
00:00:06,496 --> 00:00:11,120
happened in 11.2 and 11.3, so you can score your own capstone honestly rather than

4
00:00:11,120 --> 00:00:12,812
guessing whether it went well.

5
00:00:12,812 --> 00:00:15,969
Score each line as clear, or genuinely worth revisiting.

6
00:00:15,969 --> 00:00:19,353
Don't round up a shaky answer to make the total look better.

7
00:00:19,353 --> 00:00:23,976
The whole point of this rubric is the same honesty Section 8 asked of every result

8
00:00:23,976 --> 00:00:26,908
on Harlan's account, aimed at your own work instead.

9
00:00:26,908 --> 00:00:29,953
Do this alone, without anyone else grading it for you.

10
00:00:29,953 --> 00:00:34,295
Nobody else is going to check your capstone the way you'd check a real client

11
00:00:34,295 --> 00:00:38,637
account, which means this rubric only works if you hold yourself to the exact

12
00:00:38,637 --> 00:00:41,795
standard you'd expect from someone else's honest report.

13
00:00:42,345 --> 00:00:46,374
Work through these in order, the same order the two worked passes used.

14
00:00:46,374 --> 00:00:51,084
A weak product truth doc quietly weakens every check after it, so starting anywhere

15
00:00:51,084 --> 00:00:55,794
else risks scoring a later stage as clear when the foundation underneath it wasn't.

16
00:00:55,794 --> 00:00:58,462
"Needs work" on any line isn't a failing grade.

17
00:00:58,462 --> 00:01:03,229
It's information: go back to the specific lecture that check traces to, fix that one

18
00:01:03,229 --> 00:01:04,307
piece, and move on.

19
00:01:04,307 --> 00:01:08,961
A capstone honestly scored as three clear and two needs work is more useful to you

20
00:01:08,961 --> 00:01:12,990
than one you told yourself was five for five without actually checking.

21
00:01:12,990 --> 00:01:17,757
Notice the right-hand column points you at a specific worked moment in 11.2 or 11.3,

22
00:01:17,757 --> 00:01:19,232
not just a section number.

23
00:01:19,232 --> 00:01:23,829
If a line reads unclear, watch that exact moment again before rewriting anything.

24
00:01:23,829 --> 00:01:28,483
Seeing how the worked example handled it is usually faster than guessing at a fix.

25
00:01:29,033 --> 00:01:32,358
Fill in the "because" on every line, not just the verdict.

26
00:01:32,358 --> 00:01:37,116
"Clear" without a reason is the same unearned confidence Section 8.2 already warned

27
00:01:37,116 --> 00:01:37,804
you against.

28
00:01:37,804 --> 00:01:42,562
A real reason forces you to point at the specific evidence, not just a feeling that

29
00:01:42,562 --> 00:01:43,823
it probably went fine.

30
00:01:43,823 --> 00:01:47,836
The overall line at the bottom isn't automatic from the five above it.

31
00:01:47,836 --> 00:01:52,479
Five clears with a genuine winner, read honestly, might still mean one more cycle

32
00:01:52,479 --> 00:01:57,008
before scaling makes sense, the same patience Section 8.4 asked of Harlan's own

33
00:01:57,008 --> 00:01:59,129
refresh before it earned real budget.

34
00:01:59,129 --> 00:02:01,079
Keep this sheet, whatever it says.

35
00:02:01,079 --> 00:02:05,722
If every line comes back clear on the first try, that's a genuinely strong result

36
00:02:05,722 --> 00:02:10,308
worth being honest with yourself about too, not something to second-guess out of

37
00:02:10,308 --> 00:02:10,652
habit.

38
00:02:10,652 --> 00:02:15,353
Either way, this sheet is the actual record of where your capstone stood, and it's

39
00:02:15,353 --> 00:02:18,621
worth having on hand the next time you run a real sprint.

40
00:02:19,171 --> 00:02:23,787
Last lecture is short: where to go next, once this rubric is filled in honestly,

41
00:02:23,787 --> 00:02:24,768
whatever it says.

42
00:02:24,768 --> 00:02:28,461
If most of this came back "needs work," that's not a bad result.

43
00:02:28,461 --> 00:02:33,307
It's the same noise verdict Section 8.2 already told you to expect from a first real

44
00:02:33,307 --> 00:02:33,596
pass.

45
00:02:33,596 --> 00:02:35,904
Fix what needs fixing, and run it again.

46
00:02:35,904 --> 00:02:39,366
Would a client accept this sheet as proof of a real process?

47
00:02:39,366 --> 00:02:42,424
That's worth asking honestly before you close it out.

48
00:02:42,424 --> 00:02:47,040
It's the same question Section 10.3's case study will eventually put in front of

49
00:02:47,040 --> 00:02:51,079
someone else, and this is your chance to answer it for yourself first.
